             ["(defn f [a :string b :string] :bool (not (string=? a b)))" :ok]
             ["(defn f [a :string] :bool (and (string=? a \"x\") (string=? a \"y\")))" :ok]
             ["(defn f [a :string] :i64 (if (string=? a \"x\") 1 0))" :ok]
             ["(defn f [a :i64] :bool (if (< a 2) false (= a 3)))" :ok]]
    :backends {:measured [:aarch64-kotoba-v1 :x86_64-kotoba-v1 :wasm32-kotoba-v1
                          :cljs-kotoba-v1 :js-kotoba-v1]
               :result :all-ok
               :probe "(defn f [a :string b :string] :bool (string=? a b))"}
    :conformance-vector-migrated
    "resources/kotoba/lang-conformance/values/string_contains_kit.kotoba now converts
     explicitly at the numeric boundary ((if p 1 0)) instead of adding two predicate
     results as i64, which is what :conformance-vector-migration named."
    :breaking
    "Probe 3 flipped from :ok to :reject. A string predicate can no longer sit in an
     i64 position; that spelling must convert explicitly. This is the deprecation
     the :classification-note predicted, and it was taken rather than deferred."}
   :reason
   "`string=?` and `string-contains?` infer `:bool`, like every other predicate, so
    they compose under `and`/`or`/`not` and can be returned from a `:bool` function.
    A string predicate no longer sits in an `:i64` position -- convert explicitly."
   :history
   "Recorded :not-yet-implemented from 2026-08-10 (measured against compiler 875e388 /
    kotoba-sema 6568fb7) until 2026-08-12. Profile 5 (compiler ADR 0191) had moved
    comparisons and predicates to `:bool` so `and`/`or`/`not` compose into a readable
    predicate, carrying `= < > <= >= not not= zero? pos? neg? empty? some?` -- but not
    the two string predicates, which kept returning `:i64` while the later
    `string-index-contains` in the same file returned `:bool`. One file typed two
    spellings of the same question differently. `:consequences` below lists what that
    cost; it describes the closed period, not current behaviour."
   :consequences
   ["a :bool-declared function cannot return a string predicate"
    "(and (string=? a b) (string=? a c)) is rejected: expected bool, got i64"
    "a string predicate cannot sit opposite a true/false literal in an `if`"
    "the workaround is (if p true false), which is ADR 0191's own mechanical
     migration run backwards"]
   :still-works
   ["a string predicate in `if` test position — `if` accepts a bool or a legacy
     0/1 integer test (ADR 0191 decision 2)"
    "`not` over a string predicate — `not` desugars to (if x false true)"]
   :measured-before-closure
   {:date "2026-08-10"
    :against {:compiler "875e388" :kotoba-sema "6568fb7"}
    :superseded-by "Superseded by :closed above. Kept as the record of what the gap was."
    :probes [["(defn f [a :string b :string] :bool (string=? a b))" :reject "expected bool, got i64"]
             ["(defn f [a :string] :bool (string-contains? a \"x\"))" :reject "expected bool, got i64"]
             ["(defn f [a :string] :i64 (string-contains? a \"x\"))" :ok]
             ["(defn f [a :i64 b :i64] :bool (= a b))" :ok]
             ["(defn f [a :string b :string] :bool (not (string=? a b)))" :ok]
             ["(defn f [a :string] :bool (and (string=? a \"x\") (string=? a \"y\")))" :reject "expected bool, got i64"]
             ["(defn f [a :string] :i64 (if (string=? a \"x\") 1 0))" :ok]
             ["(defn f [a :i64] :bool (if (< a 2) false (= a 3)))" :ok]]
    :found-by "jp-go-dds kotoba/bridge_document.kotoba (com-junkawasaki ADR-2608100300)"
    :note
    "The last probe is why this is a predicate-typing gap and not an `if`
     unification gap: `=` is a call and unifies with a literal branch fine."}
   :classification-note
   "Not a safety constraint. `:classification-rule` requires a named invariant,
    fail-closed enforcement and an ADR for that disposition; this has none, and
    `:bool-is-a-type-not-a-number` argues the other way — it exists so that
    predicates compose. Closing it is a profile change with a deprecation
    window because `resources/kotoba/lang-conformance/values/string_contains_kit.kotoba`
    adds two `string-contains?` results together and asserts the i64 result;
    that vector, not the language design, is what makes this breaking.
    (2026-08-12: this reasoning held. The vector was migrated and the break was
    taken — see `:closed :breaking`.)"}
